.TH BASE64 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
base64 \- encode or decode base64
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B base64
.RB [ \-w
.IR col ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B base64 \-d
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B base64
encodes
.I file
as base64 on standard output, or with
.B \-d
decodes it.
With no
.IR file ,
or when it is
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
Encoding uses the standard alphabet of RFC 4648: uppercase letters,
lowercase letters, digits,
.BR + ,
and
.BR / .
Each 3 input bytes become 4 characters, and the output is padded with
.B =
to a multiple of 4 characters.
The output is broken into lines of
.I col
characters, set by
.BR \-w ,
and each line, including a shorter last line, ends in LF.
Empty input produces no output.
.PP
Decoding writes the bytes that the input encodes.
Characters outside the alphabet are skipped, including spaces, line
breaks, NUL bytes, and the
.B \-
and
.B _
of the URL-safe alphabet, so base64url input does not decode correctly.
An
.B =
is padding only where it completes a group of 4 characters, and is
otherwise skipped.
Decoding continues after padding, so encodings joined with line breaks
between them decode to the joined data.
If the input ends inside a group, as unpadded input may,
.B base64
writes what it has decoded and fails with
.BR "truncated input" .
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped and may follow
.IR file .
The argument of
.B \-w
may be attached, as in
.BR \-w0 ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.B \-d
Decode instead of encode.
.TP
.B \-i
Accepted and ignored.
Decoding always skips characters outside the alphabet.
.TP
.BI "\-w " col
Wrap encoded lines at
.I col
characters.
The default is 76, and 0 disables wrapping, in which case no newline at
all is written.
.I col
is a decimal number from 0 to 2147483647.
Ignored with
.BR \-d .
.TP
.B \-\-help
When it is the first argument, print a usage message on standard output
and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must precede
.IR file .
.PP
Variables that busybox-w32 reads in every applet, such as those that
control console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line,
.I file
could not be opened or read, truncated input, or an error writing
standard output.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed while
.B base64
was writing to it.
A closed pipe found only when the output is flushed at exit gives status
1 instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
Encode a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ base64 hello.txt
SGVsbG8sIHdvcmxkIQo=
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ base64 \-d hello.b64 >hello.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Encode a file as a single line with no final newline:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ base64 \-w0 icon.png >icon.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make a random 32-byte key:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ head \-c 32 /dev/urandom | base64
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU base64:
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options, such as
.B \-\-decode
or
.BR \-\-wrap ,
and no
.BR \-\-version .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-d
skips characters outside the alphabet whether or not
.B \-i
is given, where GNU fails without
.BR \-i .
.PP
Encoded lines end with LF, not CRLF.
Input is read in binary mode, so files are encoded byte for byte.
.PP
When standard output is a console, decoded data passes through the
busybox-w32 console layer as text, so binary data does not display
faithfully.
Redirect it to a file or a pipe.
.PP
Decoding reads the input in pieces that end at a character with code 32
(space) or less, or after 80 characters, and has two bugs:
.IP \(bu 2
A group of 4 characters that continues into the next piece decodes
wrongly, or fails as truncated input, when a skipped character falls
inside the group before the break.
This includes an
.B =
that ends a line before the
.B =
that starts the next, which an encoder writes for some inputs when it
wraps at an odd width, such as
.BR "\-w 3" .
The last byte of such encoded data is lost without an error.
.IP \(bu 2
Characters that follow
.B =
padding with no space or line break in between, as in
.BR YQ==YQ== ,
can fail as truncated input.
.PP
.I file
may be a Windows path, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or
a busybox-w32 device name such as
.I /dev/null
or
.IR /dev/urandom .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR base32 (1),
.BR busybox (1),
.BR uudecode (1),
.BR uuencode (1)
